WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.330 --> 00:00:03.710
Narrator: This car crash during
the "Shang-Chi" bus fight

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:07.520
was real and filmed on
location in San Francisco.

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.150
But the shots we see right after

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.940
were shot thousands of miles away

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.550
on a soundstage in Australia.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:15.970
Perfectly matching the stunts, camerawork,

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.600
and visual effects in these shots

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.270
was just one of the challenges

00:00:19.270 --> 00:00:22.280
in creating this
six-and-a-half-minute fight sequence

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.620
that took an entire year to make.

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:27.150
So how did "Shang-Chi and
the Legend of the Ten Rings"

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:30.150
pull off its unforgettable bus fight?

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.830
Since the release of "Shang-Chi,"

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.720
many have called this bus sequence

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.010
the best fight scene in
Marvel movie history.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:38.520
When putting this sequence together,

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.950
director Destin Daniel Cretton focused

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.340
on constantly raising the stakes.

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:44.780
That can be seen in everything

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:47.505
from the number of
fighters to the set itself.

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:49.050
Andy Cheng: This is
one of the big sequence

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:52.090
I think I ever worked.
With all the element.

00:00:52.090 --> 00:00:53.340
Narrator: That's Andy Cheng,

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.160
the film's fight coordinator.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.090
Doing kung fu on a moving surface

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.360
added a brand-new layer of difficulty

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.040
for the experienced stuntman.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:02.810
To handle all the moving pieces,

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.450
the action and the bus' path

00:01:04.450 --> 00:01:06.120
were mapped out thoroughly.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:07.030
How thoroughly?

00:01:07.030 --> 00:01:09.023
Well, even this shot of a passenger

00:01:09.023 --> 00:01:10.879
filming the fight on his phone

00:01:10.879 --> 00:01:13.260
was there since the storyboard phase.

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.242
And the previsualized CGI
shots of the bus' wild ride

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:19.166
provided an accurate roadmap

00:01:19.166 --> 00:01:20.949
for the stunt team to plan around,

00:01:20.949 --> 00:01:22.190
as you can see here.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.780
To really nail down the moves,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.080
the team had to rehearse on the actual bus

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.010
used in the scene, instead
of just at the gym.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.570
Cheng was inspired by a lesson

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.540
he learned while working with Jackie Chan.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:34.938
Cheng: We always have to have the sequence

00:01:34.938 --> 00:01:37.590
or storytelling with locations.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.190
Sometimes you read the script, you know,

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.210
"Oh, this is happening in this thing,"

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:42.828
but you just do it at the gym.

00:01:42.828 --> 00:01:44.912
You start pretending,
"Oh, this is a table."

00:01:44.912 --> 00:01:48.500
For me, I feel when you go
to the location and see it,

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:50.340
that's how you create things,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:52.810
because those thing is
really not pretending.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.410
Narrator: Practicing on the actual bus

00:01:54.410 --> 00:01:57.055
also allowed them to solve
some practical challenges

00:01:57.055 --> 00:01:58.805
they might not have caught earlier.

00:01:58.805 --> 00:02:02.035
For example, Florian Munteanu,
who played Razor Fist,

00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:04.300
was so tall that he kept hitting his head

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.330
on the bus ceiling during rehearsals.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:08.320
We had to constantly remind him,

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.640
"Hey, watch out! Duck down!"

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.300
Narrator: These rehearsals also identified

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.720
where they should add rubber padding

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.100
to the poles, bus floors, and chairs,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.149
which was useful here,

00:02:16.149 --> 00:02:18.740
when this fighter collapses into a seat.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.310
And to make a flip like this

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.400
happen on a set this crammed,

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.270
the team reconfigured the buses

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.210
so they could be taken apart.

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:28.350
For example, all the chairs could be moved

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.133
to make room for stuntpeople
and camera operators,

00:02:31.133 --> 00:02:32.350
and the windows could be removed

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:34.600
to allow a camera to pass through.

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:37.780
The crew also built a
sliding camera track up here,

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.000
freeing up room for hand-to-hand combat,

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.720
like in this shot, which
starts out behind Razor Fist

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.900
and then ends up in front of him.

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:47.467
After rehearsing, the
next step was figuring out

00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:51.292
how to execute a fight
scene on a moving set.

00:02:51.292 --> 00:02:53.380
Cheng wouldn't have been
able to safely achieve

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:54.980
these stunts on location,

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:56.500
as the bus speeds downhill

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:58.800
with many difficult twists and turns.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.890
So the crew constructed two bus rigs

00:03:00.890 --> 00:03:02.490
to simulate the movement.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.510
The first was built 3 feet off the ground

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:06.974
on a rig capable of moving and shaking,

00:03:06.974 --> 00:03:09.400
but it was used for more
standard driving shots

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:11.950
with a somewhat stable
surface for fighting.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:13.798
You only can make the
bus, like, this much,

00:03:13.798 --> 00:03:17.010
the tipping maybe by 10
degrees. That's maximum.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.200
Narrator: The second bus was built

00:03:18.200 --> 00:03:20.064
on a 15-foot-high gimbal

00:03:20.064 --> 00:03:23.160
capable of tilting at a 45-degree angle.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.544
It was ideal for when the
bus careens down steep hills.

00:03:26.544 --> 00:03:28.100
And because it was so high up,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:29.780
there was plenty of room for the back

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.691
to move and swing rapidly.

00:03:31.691 --> 00:03:34.299
They can be like this,
and then they can rotate,

00:03:34.299 --> 00:03:35.852
and then they have more room.

00:03:35.852 --> 00:03:37.080
They can be like this.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.910
Narrator: The bus could
even drive on an angle,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.980
like when it's wedged
between a garbage truck

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.390
and a row of cars.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.227
And the previs helped the
stunt and special effects teams

00:03:45.227 --> 00:03:48.441
adjust the gimbal's exact
movements before filming.

00:03:48.441 --> 00:03:51.270
But the most difficult
shots to get were the ones

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.900
when Shang-Chi jumps out of the bus.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.055
They had to make it look like

00:03:55.055 --> 00:03:57.240
the character was really
hitting the ground

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.300
and trying to regain his balance.

00:03:59.300 --> 00:04:01.190
So the stunt team set up a treadmill

00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:02.778
next to one of the buses.

00:04:02.778 --> 00:04:05.251
Cheng: So, in real life,
if the bus is moving,

00:04:05.251 --> 00:04:07.717
it foot touch the floor, there would be,

00:04:07.717 --> 00:04:10.387
like, constantly sliding,
because he cannot stand on it

00:04:10.387 --> 00:04:12.060
because the floor is moving.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:13.130
Narrator: The illusion wouldn't work

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:14.638
unless the bus kept moving,

00:04:14.638 --> 00:04:15.700
which is why you can see it

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:17.730
bouncing up and down off the ground.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.240
This particular moment was so complicated

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:22.760
that the stunt required both bus rigs.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.245
For the first part, the
team filmed a stunt double

00:04:25.245 --> 00:04:27.253
as Shang-Chi dodging Razor Fist

00:04:27.253 --> 00:04:29.090
and then jumping onto the roof.

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:30.780
This was done on the higher gimbal,

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.570
as it required a sharper turn.

00:04:32.570 --> 00:04:35.191
Cheng: So that one, we can
tip the bus, like 45 degree

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:37.810
and then, boom! Flip it up,
and he's on the ceiling.

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:39.070
Narrator: The second part was filmed

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:42.307
as one continuous take
that ends in a close-up,

00:04:42.307 --> 00:04:45.220
meaning Simu Liu had to
do the stunt himself.

00:04:45.220 --> 00:04:47.172
So they used the lower bus rig.

00:04:47.172 --> 00:04:49.687
Liu first positioned himself here.

00:04:49.687 --> 00:04:52.156
Less rumbling meant he could
run the length of the bus,

00:04:52.156 --> 00:04:54.110
slide, and grab the front mirror

00:04:54.110 --> 00:04:56.290
as this Technocrane followed along.

00:04:56.290 --> 00:04:59.715
Cheng estimates that they
nailed it after 10 takes.

00:04:59.715 --> 00:05:02.720
But not everything went according
to the previsualized plan.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.430
Like this shot, for example.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.322
In final rehearsals, Cretton
didn't find it believable

00:05:07.322 --> 00:05:10.030
that Shang-Chi could pin
Razor Fist against the wall

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.400
simply by putting his
foot up against this pole.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:13.758
Cheng: He's such a big guy.

00:05:13.758 --> 00:05:16.508
I cannot really buy how
he can have enough power

00:05:16.508 --> 00:05:17.966
to trap him like this.

00:05:17.966 --> 00:05:19.550
Narrator: So Cheng reshaped the scenery,

00:05:19.550 --> 00:05:21.740
switching out the pole for these chairs.

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.652
That way, Shang-Chi could place one foot

00:05:23.652 --> 00:05:25.489
against that seat and then jump up

00:05:25.489 --> 00:05:27.395
and land on Razor Fist's back.

00:05:27.395 --> 00:05:28.856
Cheng: He put one foot on the ceiling,

00:05:28.856 --> 00:05:30.974
and then against him using gravity,

00:05:30.974 --> 00:05:32.760
and all his weight on top.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:33.760
Narrator: But for Cheng,

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:36.310
this might've been the
toughest shot to get,

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:38.090
when Shang-Chi kicks Razor Fist

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:39.830
into the breakaway back of the bus

00:05:39.830 --> 00:05:42.270
before diving into the front section.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.887
To get this shot, Liu had to jump 20 feet

00:05:44.887 --> 00:05:48.524
while doing a 180-degree
turn onto the ground.

00:05:48.524 --> 00:05:50.933
And so they wouldn't have
to actually destroy the bus,

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:53.440
it was all shot in front of a blue screen.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.419
An even faster slider track

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:57.900
gave the camera enough
speed to capture the jump

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.190
while moving back to
give Liu plenty of room

00:06:00.190 --> 00:06:02.957
to land on his mark, right
in front of the lens.

00:06:02.957 --> 00:06:04.880
And then the camera moves superfast.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.050
It's almost like chasing gravity.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.050
Narrator: It took roughly 15 takes,

00:06:09.050 --> 00:06:11.070
an entire day's worth of work,

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.316
for about four seconds of footage.

00:06:13.316 --> 00:06:15.420
Now, none of these action
highlights would work

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:16.720
if the soundstage shots

00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.680
and the real shots on
location didn't match up.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.939
The two units shot simultaneously,

00:06:21.939 --> 00:06:24.085
but there were lots of
adjustments the crew made

00:06:24.085 --> 00:06:26.683
to make it feel more
organic, like lighting.

00:06:26.683 --> 00:06:30.621
Cinematographer Bill Pope built
light grids around each bus.

00:06:30.621 --> 00:06:32.017
The lights were rigged to imitate

00:06:32.017 --> 00:06:34.524
light captured on
location in San Francisco

00:06:34.524 --> 00:06:36.725
and cast accurate shadows.

00:06:36.725 --> 00:06:38.784
Notice the bus' accordion flaps moving

00:06:38.784 --> 00:06:41.180
as if they were being hit by real wind?

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.510
That's because these crew members

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.860
manually moved the flaps around.

00:06:44.860 --> 00:06:46.220
In the end, it's hard to tell

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:48.810
that this car crash was
filmed in San Francisco

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:51.356
and the shot of two
vehicles wedged together

00:06:51.356 --> 00:06:54.745
was shot on a soundstage
thousands of miles away.

00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:00.511
Cheng: Over this,
Man: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:00.511 --> 00:07:02.865
and now you come back. "Ahh!"

